requirements:
Implement in Rust as a SINGLE source file that compiles with rustc --edition 2024, std only. Output ONLY the Rust source code inside ```rust fences. All listed API items and fields must be pub. Derive Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Eq for data/error enums and records (engine structs need not implement these). No main function is required. 

Exact public API:
```rust
pub struct Job { pub id: String, pub ready_at: u64, pub priority: u8 }
pub struct Dispatch { pub id: String, pub effective_priority: u8 }
pub enum Error { TimeReversed, BadId, BadPriority, Duplicate, Unknown, Cancelled, Completed }
pub struct Scheduler { /* private fields */ }
impl Scheduler {
 pub fn new(aging_ms: u64) -> Self;
 pub fn insert(&mut self, now: u64, job: Job) -> Result<(), Error>;
 pub fn cancel(&mut self, now: u64, id: &str) -> Result<(), Error>;
 pub fn next_ready(&mut self, now: u64) -> Result<Option<Dispatch>, Error>;
}
```

R1. Time starts at 0. Every successful operation records now, even next_ready returning None. A call with now less than the last successful time returns TimeReversed before any other checks.
R2. An error changes nothing, including the clock, queue, and insertion sequence.
R3. Job IDs contain 1 through 16 ASCII bytes, each a lowercase letter, digit, or hyphen. insert validates ID before priority before duplication.
R4. Priorities must be in 0..=9; otherwise insert returns BadPriority.
R5. An ID is reserved forever after insertion; inserting it again returns Duplicate, even after cancellation or dispatch.
R6. insert accepts any ready_at, including past times and u64::MAX. Successful insert assigns a strictly increasing FIFO sequence.
R7. cancel checks time first, then looks up the exact ID without validating its syntax; an absent ID returns Unknown.
R8. Cancelling a queued job succeeds, including a job whose ready_at is in the future.
R9. Cancelling an already cancelled job returns Cancelled.
R10. Cancelling an already dispatched job returns Completed.
R11. next_ready considers only queued jobs with ready_at <= now.
R12. With aging_ms=0, effective priority equals original priority.
R13. Otherwise effective priority is min(9, original priority + floor((now-ready_at)/aging_ms)). Compute this mathematically without integer overflow.
R14. Choose highest effective priority. On ties choose earliest successful insertion, regardless of ready_at or original priority.
R15. Dispatch removes the selected job from the queue permanently and returns its ID and effective priority.
R16. When no eligible job exists return Ok(None); cancelled jobs never become eligible.
R17. Future jobs gain no aging before readiness; aging is based on ready_at, not insertion time.
R18. Each scheduler is independent. There is no background clock or implicit time advance.
